// The child's ENTRY path (#4060) — the counterpart of ./spawn.bit's
// `threadEpilogue`, and a sibling file rather than more of `spawn.bit` because
// that file is 742 lines and the 800-line ceiling is a ceiling.
//
// WHAT THIS ESTABLISHES, because it is a soundness property and not a nicety.
// `bit_rt_gc_thread_enter` claims this OS thread's mutator slot and publishes
// its real stack top and base (ABI.md §5, `../../gc/gcworldsync.bit`'s
// `mutatorSlot`). Two consumers need that: `worldStoppedFor`'s stop-the-world
// rendezvous, which counts an UNCLAIMED slot as stopped and therefore never
// waits for the thread, and `stwScanOwnStack`, which needs the published range
// to scan it. Until the claim happens this thread is invisible to both, so an
// object it becomes the sole holder of is swept underneath it — ABI.md §5
// records #1677's reproducer doing exactly that, 20/20 runs.
//
// IT USED TO HAPPEN LAZILY, ON EVERY ALLOCATION. `../../gc/gcalloc.bit`'s
// `gcAlloc`/`gcAllocRaw` opened with a `registerAllocator()` call, so the fact
// was re-established once per OBJECT — a `bl` into `mutatorSlot`, whose
// prologue spills x19-x28, plus one `ldar` and one `bl
// bit_rt_port_gc_world_addr` beside it — to maintain something that changes
// exactly once in a thread's life. Measured on `bench/cases/alloc`: 79
// instructions and 11-15 cycles per object. #4060 moved it here.
//
// THE OBLIGATION BELONGS TO THE PROVIDER, NOT THE CHILD BODY, for the reason
// ./spawn.bit's exit half already states at length: asking "user code that
// starts a raw OS thread" to make the call is a rule every future caller has
// to remember, and the penalty for forgetting is silent. This provider owns
// the child's entry, so it is the one place that can discharge it
// unconditionally.
//
// Declared directly rather than imported from `../../gc`, same as the exit
// call: the live registry in a normally linked program is `runtime/root/`'s,
// not the Bit module's, whose `worldAddr` is bound only by `runtime/root`'s
// own boot. The symbol IS in the linked archive (E0078 admits it, #1444).
extern fn bit_rt_gc_thread_enter()

// Call the raw code address `entry` with `arg` in the first-argument register
// and return normally. A copy of ../darwin/spawn.bit's own copy of
// ../../sched's `taskInvoke`, for the same leaf reason and with the same
// encoding — `entry` is a bare code pointer, not a `(code, env)` function
// value, so no `call_value` reaches it, and a provider may not import
// `../../sched`.
//
// The clobber list names every caller-saved GPR a C-ABI callee may destroy,
// not just the link register (#3916): `threadChildRun`'s `addr` is live across
// this call, and an under-declared list lets the allocator park it in a
// register the callee is free to overwrite. That was live and measured on the
// Darwin twin.
@nosplit fn invokeRaw(entry: int, arg: int) {
  asm {
    arm64 { 0xD63F0120 } // blr x9
    x64 { 0xFF, 0xD0 }   // call rax
    input arm64 x9 x64 rax = entry
    input arm64 x0 x64 rdi = arg
    clobber arm64 {
      x1,
      x2,
      x3,
      x4,
      x5,
      x6,
      x7,
      x8,
      x10,
      x11,
      x12,
      x13,
      x14,
      x15,
      x16,
      x17,
      x18,
      x30,
      memory,
    }
    clobber x64 { rcx, rdx, rsi, r8, r9, r10, r11, memory }
  }
}

// `int` -> `*byte`, for handing the exit cell's address to the child as its one
// argument. Same nil-base construction as ./spawn.bit's `i32Ptr` and
// ./tls.bit's word helpers, spelled here because a leaf may not import one.
fn bytesAt(addr: int): *byte {
  let z: *byte = nil
  return z + addr
}

// Every OS thread this provider starts enters here, not at the caller's body —
// the shape ../darwin/spawn.bit and ../windows/spawn.bit already have.
//
// THE HAND-ENCODED CHILD SEQUENCE IN `cloneChild` IS UNCHANGED BY THIS, and
// that is the whole reason this shape was chosen over editing it. The child
// still does `mov x0, <arg>; blr <entry>` (`mov rdi, ...; call ...` on x64);
// only the two values the PARENT puts in those registers moved.
// `threadStart` now passes `entryOf(threadChildRun)` as the entry and the exit
// cell's address as the arg, and the caller's real pair is parked in the cell
// at `childEntryOffset()`/`childArgOffset()`. No instruction encoding and no
// branch displacement changes, on either architecture.
//
// NOT `@nosplit`, because that attribute forbids the `extern` call this exists
// to make — the same reason ./spawn.bit's `threadEpilogue` is not. It contains
// no loop, so codegen emits no back-edge safepoint in it: there is no window
// between the claim and the body in which this thread could park with no
// snapshot published.
//
// The return value is discarded by the `asm` that called us; it exists to
// match the `(*byte) => *byte` shape `entryOf` takes here.
fn threadChildRun(cell: *byte): *byte {
  // FIRST, before any other Bit statement this thread runs.
  bit_rt_gc_thread_enter()
  let addr = int(cell)
  invokeRaw(loadWord(addr + childEntryOffset()), loadWord(addr + childArgOffset()))
  let z: *byte = nil
  return z
}
